WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.890
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:06.660
Today I want to talk about how
long it takes to learn a language.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.220
It's a question I get all the time.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.390
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.100
on the bell to get notifications.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.960
And of course, by all means, join me at
LingQ, which is where I learn languages.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.480
So I get this question quite often,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.960
how long does it take to
learn languages in general?

00:00:25.935 --> 00:00:28.845
Which is a very difficult question
to answer, or how long does it

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:32.055
take to learn Spanish or Chinese?

00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:36.345
So my first reaction is, what's the hurry.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.190
And I'll explain why.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:45.240
Now there are some evaluations
by authoratative, uh, sources

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.050
of information like the American
Foreign Services Institute,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.380
and I'll leave a link in the
description box and you can look up

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.629
how many instructional hours they
think is necessary, or based on their

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:02.129
experience is necessary, for an English
speaker to learn different languages.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.240
And you will see there that
obviously languages that are closer

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.670
to English that share vocabulary
with English, uh, are easier, you

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.650
know, French, Spanish, and so forth,

00:01:13.679 --> 00:01:14.280
even German.

00:01:14.789 --> 00:01:19.650
Languages that are more different from
English take significantly longer.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.850
And there's an indication there
of how many hours are required.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.320
Now I have a few problems with that
and I'm going to go through those.

00:01:28.664 --> 00:01:31.645
First problem is their evalua...

00:01:31.664 --> 00:01:34.574
or their, uh, you know,
estimate of how long it takes

00:01:34.604 --> 00:01:36.794
is based on instructional hours.

00:01:37.485 --> 00:01:42.044
All of my experience in language
learning is that what you do outside the

00:01:42.044 --> 00:01:48.645
classroom is, or can be, more important
than what you do in the classroom.

00:01:48.824 --> 00:01:56.324
Because as MAnfred Spitzer famously
said, got his book here, uh,

00:01:56.324 --> 00:02:00.494
language learning takes place in
our brains, not in the classroom.

00:02:00.615 --> 00:02:05.535
So just looking at instructional hours
to me is not a good indication of how

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:07.755
long it takes to learn a language.

00:02:08.115 --> 00:02:14.565
So that's point number one, point
number two is, you know, to me, language

00:02:14.565 --> 00:02:17.144
learning is a continuum, you know?

00:02:17.174 --> 00:02:21.045
Yes you start at a certain
point, but you never finish.

00:02:21.885 --> 00:02:27.170
Um, There is no language that I speak,
other than English let's say where

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:32.120
I don't worry about mistakes I make,
but there is no sort of second, third,

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.600
fourth, fifth language that I speak
as well as I would like to speak it.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.910
I make mistakes in all of them.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:39.890
So, but I enjoy them.

00:02:40.665 --> 00:02:45.105
So, you know, how long does it take
to achieve what becomes the question?

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:50.954
And if you enjoy the process of language
learning as I do, does it really matter?

00:02:51.015 --> 00:02:53.625
You will learn as quickly as you learn.

00:02:54.315 --> 00:02:57.734
I think for example, of my wife
who enjoys playing the piano,

00:02:57.945 --> 00:02:59.535
she's been at it for years.

00:02:59.535 --> 00:03:00.855
I don't know, 20 years.

00:03:00.975 --> 00:03:02.234
She learns on her own.

00:03:02.355 --> 00:03:03.465
She enjoys it.

00:03:03.555 --> 00:03:05.234
I hear her music in the house.

00:03:05.234 --> 00:03:05.865
I'm happy.

00:03:05.865 --> 00:03:06.975
I enjoy listening to it.

00:03:07.334 --> 00:03:08.775
She's no concert pianist.

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:14.100
She is slowly improving, but the
whole process to her is enjoyable.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.929
Otherwise she wouldn't do it.

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:20.760
I'm much the same, even in languages,
the most recent languages that I'm

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.829
learning like Persian and Arabic.

00:03:23.549 --> 00:03:25.260
I've been at it now for quite a while.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:26.070
It's amazing.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.959
It's it's two, three years.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:34.020
Um, I enjoy listening to those simple
mini stories and picking up on something,

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.220
picking something out, noticing
something that I hadn't noticed before.

00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:43.550
I enjoy the sensation of being able
to understand a podcast in Arabic or a

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.970
story about Iranian cuisine in Persian.

00:03:47.895 --> 00:03:48.735
But I'm a long way...

00:03:48.735 --> 00:03:53.205
and I enjoy, if I happen to find a
checkout person at the supermarket who

00:03:53.205 --> 00:03:57.675
speaks Arabic or Persian, and I can speak
to them in that language, I'm very happy,

00:03:57.765 --> 00:03:59.595
but I'm enjoying the whole process.

00:03:59.715 --> 00:04:03.345
And I am still a long
way from being fluent.

00:04:04.395 --> 00:04:09.555
Even that term "fluent" is a little
bit subjective because some people

00:04:09.915 --> 00:04:11.355
are motivated to want to speak

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.520
and to say even simple things,
even if they don't understand

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.690
what the other person is saying.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.430
I enjoy what I'm able to do in Arabic
and Persian, which is listening

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.079
to things and reading things.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.410
There aren't that many people that I
can speak to, I'm not going to spend

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:31.650
my evenings out, looking for people
that I can inflict myself onto so that

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.600
I can practice my Persian and Arabic.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.110
So it depends also on what our goals are.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.920
I was in some discussion and the
people who were involved in language

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:46.425
instruction were talking about
interlanguage, which is a concept that

00:04:46.425 --> 00:04:51.465
language teachers or linguists of the
academic variety have come up with.

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:58.155
And it sort of refers to the fact that
as we learn a language, you know, when we

00:04:58.155 --> 00:05:03.045
begin we're, we're very much dominated by
our native language or a language we know

00:05:03.045 --> 00:05:08.685
well, and we can gradually transition into
a situation where we more naturally use

00:05:09.044 --> 00:05:09.945
the target language.

00:05:10.275 --> 00:05:16.664
So while we're in that transitional
period, the, the sort of academic, the

00:05:16.664 --> 00:05:22.215
teachers, they talk about interlanguage
where the native language is influencing

00:05:22.275 --> 00:05:26.864
the language that we're trying to learn,
but that's almost always the case.

00:05:26.984 --> 00:05:30.405
There's nothing special about that
because the whole process of learning

00:05:30.405 --> 00:05:36.604
a language is to gradually transition
to a situation where you can naturally

00:05:36.885 --> 00:05:40.350
think a naturally use the new language.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:47.235
I mean, uh, I have always done business
with non-native speakers of English.

00:05:47.265 --> 00:05:50.715
They have the trace of
their native language.

00:05:50.715 --> 00:05:55.635
So Germans say "I have been living
in, uh, Canada since many years"

00:05:55.665 --> 00:05:56.955
which of course is not correct.

00:05:57.135 --> 00:05:59.775
English speakers speaking
French, they have trouble,

00:05:59.865 --> 00:06:03.645
even those who speak very well, they
make mistakes in gender, for example,

00:06:03.855 --> 00:06:06.945
or they'll make other mistakes
that reflect their native language.

00:06:07.005 --> 00:06:08.145
It was quite amusing to me...

00:06:08.145 --> 00:06:10.575
i, again, I follow
different people on Twitter.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.020
And there's one Twitter account, which
talks, uh, which has something to do with

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:21.200
modern foreign language instruction in
the UK, and apparently in the UK for what

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:27.110
they call the GCSE, which is a general
certificate for secondary education,

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:33.140
they have come up with a list of
words that someone should know and

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:38.060
different sort of intricacies of grammar
that people should be tested on in

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.075
order to get their GCSE certificate.

00:06:42.344 --> 00:06:49.145
One of which was "joue du" and
"joue au", so "joue du"/ "joue au".

00:06:49.145 --> 00:06:57.344
In French when you play, when you play
an instrument it's "joue du piano".

00:06:57.735 --> 00:07:02.984
When you play a sport, "joue au
football", whatever, it's "au".

00:07:03.025 --> 00:07:04.044
Ah, okay.

00:07:04.605 --> 00:07:05.865
Minor point.

00:07:05.895 --> 00:07:11.280
So you're gonna really mark down a
student who communicates well, who

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:16.590
understands well, because they didn't
touch on these specific grammar points

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:22.470
that some bureaucrat has decided
should be the criteria for, uh, either

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.300
passing someone or not passing someon?

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:25.530
It's ridiculous.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.830
Learning a language is a continuum.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.410
Hopefully we can enjoy it.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:37.425
Uh, the objective is comprehension,
to understand more and more to acquire

00:07:37.425 --> 00:07:42.825
more and more words to gradually get
enough confidence in the language.

00:07:42.854 --> 00:07:45.405
So then we can have
meaningful conversations.

00:07:45.435 --> 00:07:49.335
So, but if people are looking for
some kind of rule of thumb, some

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:54.435
kind of, um, you know, encouragement
that it's worth the effort,

00:07:54.735 --> 00:07:59.685
my experience has been that for languages
that are similar to ours with the same

00:07:59.685 --> 00:08:05.610
writing system, with a lot of common
vocabulary and if we're motivated, whether

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:09.900
we're in a classroom or learning on our
own, I think that in three months you

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.240
can make a breakthrough, three months.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:16.890
On the other hand, if we're dealing
with a language where the writing

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:21.630
system is different, in the case of
say Chinese or Arabic or Hebrew, or,

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.210
uh, you know, Korean, very different.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:30.375
If there's essentially no common
vocabulary, then to reach that sort

00:08:30.375 --> 00:08:33.225
of breakthrough point where you feel
now I've really achieved something

00:08:33.314 --> 00:08:35.835
and I'm not talking about just being
able to say a few things, you can

00:08:35.835 --> 00:08:40.275
do that after a couple of days, I'm
saying you have enough of a sense....

00:08:40.275 --> 00:08:42.194
now, maybe you understand
some of the mini stories.

00:08:42.194 --> 00:08:44.295
You have that confidence:
I'm going to make it.

00:08:44.745 --> 00:08:47.954
That can take six to nine months
in these more difficult languages.

00:08:48.345 --> 00:08:51.465
And it can seem at times as
if we're not getting anywhere.

00:08:51.495 --> 00:08:56.835
And I can remember in Russian in
Czech now in Arabic, in Persian, it

00:08:56.835 --> 00:09:03.000
seems that I'm forever, you know,
treading water, spinning my wheels.

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:04.650
And yet that's not true.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.550
If we continue with the
language, we eventually improve.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.130
And so the, the key is to enjoy
the process, to be satisfied with

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:15.719
whatever level you have achieved.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:23.490
Now, obviously, if you have to pass the
GCSE then, you know, in addition to your

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:27.120
reading and listening and enjoyable study
of the language, you may want to get a

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.290
hold of a list of the particular points of
grammar that they're going to test you on.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:36.300
Or the particular, the particular
vocabulary list, the TOEFL list, but

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.490
by and large, the learning pro...

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:42.360
and that's specific only for the exam,
because when it comes to learning the

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:47.130
language in order to be able to use the
language, to communicate with people, the

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:53.280
key is not to worry about how long does
it take but rather to find a way to enjoy

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:57.960
the language, to engage with enjoyable
content, to give yourself credit for what

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.620
you have achieved and simply stay with it.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:05.000
And you will continue to improve as
long as you continue doing those things.

